Johnny Castaway was the standout screensaver of the early 1990s, and for decades, that is where he remained. Released by Dynamix and Sierra On-Line in 1992, he fished, slept, built rafts, and got into trouble whenever an office PC sat idle. Over time, enjoying that casual rhythm became nearly impossible without dragging a dusty computer out of a closet or wrestling with an emulated virtual machine. Because the friction was so high, most people simply moved on.

I thought that with the aid of modern tooling, there had to be a way to make the island enjoyable again: [Johnny now runs directly in a browser tab](https://khawkins98.github.io/johnny_web/). Pulling that off was only possible with AI. While using LLMs to reverse-engineer legacy software has become popular recently, my efforts here predated that wave, and it was far from easy. It turned into a deep learning experience: decoding the bytecode engine, reconstructing the original executable's lost scene director, and building an automated oracle to verify the browser port against the 1992 program running in an instrumented emulator.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - Johnny's island combines scripted gags and weighted random choices with an eleven-day story that advances at most one step per calendar day.
> - The browser port builds on earlier reverse-engineering work and interprets the original game resources in JavaScript.
> - Running the original Windows program under instrumentation provides reference traces; separate rendering “goldens” catch changes in the port.

## Inside a gag, and between gags

The original files contain artwork, sound, and small programs describing the animations. They use the **Dynamix Game Development System**, or DGDS. The browser engine, which I've called Bottle DGDS, decodes those resources and runs their instructions in JavaScript.

There are two distinct script levels:

- **ADS scripts coordinate a gag.** They start and stop animations, make choices, and wait for other actions to finish.
- **TTM scripts perform the animation.** They load images, draw sprite frames, wait, play sounds, and loop or finish.

The [“Eight Arm Menace” scene flow](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/scene-flows/FISHING.ADS.md#eight-arm-menace-fishingads-tag-3) makes that concrete: Johnny gets his pole, casts it, reels in a heavy catch, and reaches the octopus gag. Several animations can be active together. Their order and timing determine whether the result reads as one coherent joke.

Choosing which gag comes next is another job entirely. The original Windows executable had a director wrapped around those scripts. It chose an eligible final scene, filled in compatible everyday scenes before it, and arranged Johnny's walks between their starting and ending positions. The browser port reconstructs that [scene-selection behaviour](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/johnny-host-behavior.md) separately from the script interpreter.

That distinction exposed a gap in earlier web ports: they could decode and play scenes that ordinary playback never selected. The files were present, but the director was missing. Decoding an animation tells you how to draw it, but not when the character should decide to walk across the sand.

## Asking the original what it actually does

I started with [Alexandre Fontoura's Castaway](https://github.com/xesf/castaway), which already had a JavaScript foundation for decoding the resources. Jérémie Guillaume's [Johnny Reborn](https://github.com/jno6809/jc_reborn), Hans Milling's [C# remake](https://github.com/nivs1978/Johnny-Castaway-Open-Source), and the [ScummVM DGDS engine](https://github.com/scummvm/scummvm/tree/master/engines/dgds) provided further findings and behavioural references. This project owes a great deal to their work.

Decoding the files gives you instructions to interpret. Examining the executable helps recover the rules around them. An interpretation can still be wrong in ways that look plausible on screen.

The useful reference is the original program running. The project's [capture tools](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/tools/faithfulness-oracle/METHODOLOGY.md) use Windows 3.1 inside a patched DOSBox-X. The patch lets the harness isolate a gag and record its animation threads over time. The captures use the same game-data version as the browser port, so differences aren't caused by comparing two releases.

